tion, Scope, Acceptance Criteria, HLR Traceability (exact-ish; validator matches heading text containing aliases after stripping non-alpha). Use \"## Description\", \"## Scope\", \"## Acceptance Criteria\", \"## HLR Traceability\".\n\nE001 body:\n\nTitle: \"Validate workspace-resolution initialization dependencies for the artifacts-only server context\"\n\nDescription: Investigation resolving whether workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins (and the kubernetes:// workspace store) can initialize and answer experiment-to-workspace lookups in a server context where tracking and model registry stores are not initialized. The strategy flags this as an open question and an assumption needing validation; the answer determines whether the plugin requires refactoring before the workspace-aware artifacts-only server mode can be built, and shapes the server's initialization sequence.\n\nScope:\n- Trace the initialization path of the kubernetes-auth plugin's workspace resolution (registered via the mlflow.workspace_provider entry point group and loaded by WorkspaceStoreRegistry) and the kubernetes:// workspace store.\n- Identify every dependency of that path on tracking store / model registry store initialization.\n- Determine whether experiment-to-workspace mapping is answerable without tracking stores (strategy assumption).\n- Determine whether artifact request paths under the S3 artifact store structure ({experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/) carry sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership.\n- If coupling is found: produce a refactor plan factoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin, listing the interfaces the server and plugin will use.\n- Deliverable: written findings with code-level evidence and a reproducible minimal harness/test demonstrating resolution with tracking stores uninitialized.\n\nHmm wait — should the artifact-path-sufficiency question be in E001 or E004? It's an assumption \"needs validation\" that affects the server's request-validation design. It's closely tied to workspace resolution (resolving path → experiment → workspace). I put it in E001 — reasonable: it's a genuine unknown about whether resolution from artifact paths is possible. Does its answer change epic structure? If paths are insufficient... the whole approach changes (rewrite). Including it in E001 keeps one investigation for related resolution unknowns (\"if two qualifying unknowns would be resolved by the same experiment... combine\"). Same experiment area. OK.\n\nACs:\n- Findings document states, with code-level evidence (file/function call paths), whether workspace resolution initialization depends on tracking or model registry store initialization, and whether experiment-to-workspace mapping is accessible without tracking stores.\n- A reproducible minimal harness (or automated test) demonstrates workspace resolution succeeding or failing in a context with no tracking store initialized — pass/fail is observable, not asserted.\n- If coupled: the refactor plan specifies the independent initialization path interfaces and the changes needed in the plugin (and any server-side hooks), sufficient to scope the follow-on implementation without re-analysis.\n- If artifact request paths are insufficient to resolve workspace membership for the S3 artifact store structure, the findings state so explicitly and identify what additional context the request path must carry.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-7 (P1) — resolves the unknown gating plugin workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context; also validates assumptions underpinning HLR-1/HLR-2 (P0).\n\nE002 body:\n\nTitle: \"Determine artifact traffic routing approach for the split-server MLflow topology\"\n\nDescription: Investigation resolving how the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to a dedicated artifacts-only instance: purely at the Gateway API HTTPRoute layer, or via a tracking-server configuration change that delegates artifact operations. Strategy open question owned by the MLflow Team; the answer determines the HTTPRoute design and whether additional tracking-server work exists.\n\nScope:\n- Enumerate the artifact-serving API surfaces (e.g. /api/2.0/mlflow/artifacts/* endpoints and artifact-related UI paths) that must be routed to the dedicated instance.\n- Evaluate routing options against the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern and conditional Gateway API CRD discovery.\n- Determine auth passthrough implications: kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, Kubernetes service account bearer tokens, X-MLFLOW-WORKSPACE header.\n- Decide: HTTPRoute-level routing suffices vs tracking server needs a delegation configuration change.\n- Sketch the HTTPRoute design for the artifacts-only instance consistent with conditional CRD discovery.\n\nACs:\n- A decision document enumerates the artifact-serving endpoints to be routed and selects one routing approach, with evidence from the operator codebase (routing/Helm chart structure) and the tracking server's endpoint surface.\n- The decision explicitly states whether the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations, and if so, what configuration surface is needed.\n- The HTTPRoute design sketch is consistent with the operator's conditional CRD discovery pattern for optional Gateway API dependencies.\n- Auth passthrough (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, service account tokens, workspace header) is addressed: the selected approach preserves the unchanged authentication setup.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-6 (P1) — determines the mechanism by which the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance; shapes HLR-5 HTTPRoute design.\n\nE003 (plugin impl):\n\nTitle: \"Workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins for the artifacts-only server context\"\n\nDescription: Make the auth plugin's workspace resolution function correctly in an artifacts-only MLflow server context where tracking and model registry stores are not initialized. Where the initialization-dependency investigation found coupling to the full tracking server initialization path, factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n\nScope:\n- Extend/verify the plugin's experiment-to-workspace resolution so it can be invoked from artifact request handling given only the workspace store (kubernetes://) — no tracking store or model registry store initialization.\n- If coupled (per investigation outcome): refactor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin, preserving the existing tracking-server behavior.\n- Support resolution from artifact request paths whose parent experiment is identifiable (S3 artifact store path structure {experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/).\n- Reuse the existing auth plugin infrastructure; no changes to the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar or --app-name kubernetes-auth authentication setup (Staff Engineer input: the strategy must not dictate Kubernetes API mechanics — SSAR-level detail is an implementation detail of the plugin, not strategy scope).\n- Automated tests covering resolution in the no-tracking-store context and regression tests for existing tracking-server resolution.\n\nACs:\n- Workspace resolution returns the correct workspace assignment for an experiment via the kubernetes:// workspace store in a server context with no tracking or model registry store initialized, demonstrated by automated tests.\n- Existing tracking-server workspace resolution behavior is unchanged (regression tests pass; no changes to authentication setup).\n- If refactored: the independent initialization path is exercised by tests that initialize resolution without any tracking store.\n- Resolution is invocable given an artifact request path's parent experiment identifier.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-7 (P1). Priority note: P0 by inheritance — enables the P0 workspace-aware artifacts-only server mode (HLR-1..4).\n\nE004 (server):\n\nTitle: \"Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\"\n\nDescription: Make --artifacts-only composable with --enable-workspaces in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit --workspace-store-uri, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and denies artifact requests that fail workspace validation. No new server flag is introduced (Staff Engineer input).\n\nScope:\n- Flag composition: allow --artifacts-only together with --enable-workspaces; require --workspace-store-uri when --enable-workspaces is set (fail to start with a clear error otherwise — per strategy dependency note); tracking and model registry stores stay uninitialized; non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (initial scope: S3 artifact store path structure {experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; deny with HTTP 403 on failed workspace validation.\n- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active and the workspace store is unreachable, deny requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation unavailable) rather than serving without validation.\n- Backwards compatibility: --artifacts-only without --enable-workspaces retains current behavior; no existing CLI arguments or configuration keys change meaning; single-tenant deployments unaffected.\n- Audit logging of workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (HLR-8, P2 — bundled here: same denial code path as the HTTP 403 handling; a separate epic would touch the same request-handling code twice).\n- Short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path (risk mitigation; the PM/Engineering latency-target decision may re-scope this — see Health Warnings).\n- Document limitations for non-standard artifact roots (risk mitigation).\n\nACs:\n- Server started with --artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// starts successfully; started with --artifacts-only --enable-workspaces without --workspace-store-uri it fails to start with a clear error.\n- With both flags set, tracking and model registry stores are not initialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with correct artifact content.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- With the workspace store unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n- With --artifacts-only and no --enable-workspaces, any authenticated user receives HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved).\n- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in server logs.\n- Workspace membership decisions are cached with a short TTL; per-request resolution does not perform an unbounded workspace store lookup per request. Hmm — \"measured by\"? Keep as is, testable via tests.\n- Automated regression tests confirm no change in behavior for configurations without workspace flags.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-1 (P0), HLR-2 (P0), HLR-3 (P0), HLR-4 (P0), HLR-8 (P2, bundled — audit logging rides the same denial path as HLR-2; splitting would audit the same call sites twice and the telemetry-channel decision is still open).\n\nE005 (operator):\n\nTitle: \"Split-server topology in the MLflow operator: dedicated artifacts-only instance\"\n\nDescription: Extend the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, and configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to that instance — a split-server topology, not a single-deployment configuration toggle (Staff Engineer input).\n\nScope:\n- New Helm chart templates (within the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern: CR spec → Helm values → rendered templates → Server-Side Apply) for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute.\n- Propagate workspace configuration (--enable-workspaces, --workspace-store-uri, --app-name kubernetes-auth) to the artifacts-only instance using the same configuration mechanisms as the main tracking deployment.\n- Configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to the dedicated instance per the routing approach selected by the routing investigation (HTTPRoute-level routing, or server-side delegation if that outcome is selected).\n- HTTPRoute resources created conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery (existing pattern for optional dependencies).\n- Deploy the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking deployment.\n- Operator validation rejecting configurations where the artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags (risk mitigation: misconfiguration must not silently bypass workspace validation).\n- Early validation that the chart architecture supports a second Deployment without structural changes (strategy assumption); if structural changes are required, re-plan within this epic before proceeding.\n- Users configure a single MLflow CR; the operator manages the split topology declaratively.\n\nACs:\n- Reconciling an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute distinct from the main tracking Deployment (verified by presence of distinct rendered resources).\n- The artifacts-only Deployment's container command includes --artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts (verified by inspecting the rendered Deployment spec).\n- The main tracking deployment's configuration routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance per the selected routing approach.\n- HTTPRoute creation follows the operator's conditional CRD discovery pattern (no hard dependency on Gateway API).\n- The operator rejects MLflow CR configurations that enable the split topology without workspace flags on the artifacts-only instance.\n- The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar is present on the artifacts-only Deployment, matching the main tracking deployment's setup.\n- End-to-end verification against a server image supporting workspace-aware artifacts-only serving: artifact requests through the dedicated instance return HTTP 200 for workspace members and HTTP 403 for non-members (this AC gates the first end-to-end execution; the operator work itself proceeds in parallel with the server-side capability — the flag combination is a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact).\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-5 (P1), HLR-6 (P1).\n\nBRANCH-B-E006:\n\nTitle: \"Tracking-server artifact traffic delegation to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\"\n\nDescription: Conditional epic (branch B of the routing investigation): implemented only if the routing approach determination concludes that Gateway API HTTPRoute-level routing alone cannot route artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, and the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations.\n\nScope:\n- Implement the tracking-server configuration change that delegates artifact operations to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, per the mechanism identified by the routing determination.\n- Preserve the single MLflow CR as the configuration surface: the delegation configuration is managed by the operator's rendering, not manual per-resource management.\n- Ensure delegated artifact traffic retains workspace validation and the unchanged authentication setup.\n\nACs:\n- The tracking server, configured per the delegation mechanism, routes artifact operations to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n- Artifact requests delegated from the main tracking server are workspace-validated at the dedicated instance (HTTP 200 for members, 403 for non-members).\n- No existing CLI arguments or configuration keys change meaning for deployments not using the split topology.\n- The delegation configuration is rendered/managed by the operator's existing configuration mechanisms (single CR surface).\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-6 (P1) — conditional share of the artifact-traffic-routing requirement.\n\nBranch frontmatter: branch=B, gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8009-E002, dependencies=RHAISTRAT-8009-E002, gate_failure_impact.action=remove, fallback_approach=\"If HTTPRoute-level routing suffices (outcome A), this epic is removed; artifact traffic routing is configured entirely by the operator's split-server topology work.\"\n\nWait — gate_failure_impact semantics: impact if the gate (investigation outcome) fails to match this branch. For branch B epic: if outcome B does not hold → epic removed. action=remove. Good.\n\nNote: branch epic deps only E002 (no main-plan E004 dep, per branch rules). But its ACs require the artifacts-only capability... fine, AC gates.\n\nHmm, one concern: branch-B epic priority P1, and validator check 4: for P0 epics' closures — branch epics skipped. OK.\n\nAlso: does the branch epic share component MLflow with other non-docs Implementation epics and have an enforcement-y title? Title \"Tracking-server artifact traffic delegation...\" — no enforcement words. OK.\n\nNow signals rationale files. Format: markdown table with Signal, Value, Rationale.\n\nE001 investigation_signals:\n- question_specificity: 1 — concrete, enumerable questions (does workspace resolution init depend on tracking store init; is mapping accessible without stores; do S3 artifact paths carry exp