{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-16ce3819-7ef6-41ab-b083-2613125e3fa2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fe51066705f90f8","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-6bd4guuy/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009 (Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments)\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Validate workspace-resolution initialization dependencies for artifacts-only serving | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Determine artifact traffic routing approach for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Plugin workspace resolution for the artifacts-only server context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | MLflow operator split-server topology for dedicated artifacts-only serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006 | Tracking-server artifact traffic delegation to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation (branch B, conditional) | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\nMain plan: 5 epics (E001–E005) plus 1 conditional branch epic. `epic_count=6` counts all epics including the branch.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigation: workspace-resolution init dependencies\"] --> E003[\"E003 Plugin workspace resolution (artifacts-only context)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 Investigation: artifact traffic routing approach\"] --> E005[\"E005 Operator split-server topology\"]\n    E003 --> E004[\"E004 Workspace-aware artifacts-only server mode\"]\n    E002 -.->|\"conditional (outcome B only)\"| BRANCHB[\"BRANCH-B-E006 Tracking-server delegation\"]\n```\n\nArrows point from dependency to dependent (`E001 --> E003` = E001 must complete before E003 starts).\n\nMain-plan critical path: E001 → E003 → E004 (length 3). E002 → E005 runs in parallel with the server-side chain. Branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The initialization-dependency investigation determines whether plugin workspace resolution must be refactored (coupled to tracking-store init) before it can function in the artifacts-only context — a true gate; `gate_failure_impact.action=add_remediation`. Bounded outcomes (coupled / not coupled) are both absorbed into E003's scope (\"verify and extend\", refactor path prescribed by the strategy's risk mitigation), so no branch epics are needed for this gate. |\n| E001 → E004 (transitive via E003) | Rule 3 | The server's workspace-provider initialization approach depends on the same unknown; covered transitively — no redundant direct edge. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The routing-approach investigation determines the HTTPRoute design and whether tracking-server delegation work exists — a true gate; `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (library → consumer) | `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` is a library the tracking server loads; the server's artifact-request validation builds against the plugin's workspace-resolution capability extended by E003. Consumer is serial after the library change. This matches the strategy's dependency table (\"auth plugin must support workspace resolution … in a server context where tracking stores are not initialized\"). |\n| E005 ∥ E003, E005 ∥ E004 (no edge) | Rule 13 carve-out + Rule 14 | Different repos (mlflow-operator vs mlflow), no shared build artifact. The flag combination the operator renders is a **configuration reference**, not a build-against artifact — the coupling is expressed as an end-to-end AC gate on E005, never as a DAG edge. |\n| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | — | Independent unknowns with different deliverables; both start immediately. |\n| E002 -.-> BRANCH-B-E006 | Conditional decomposition | The routing question has two bounded outcomes. Outcome A (HTTPRoute-level routing suffices) adds no epic — main-plan E005 covers it. Outcome B (tracking server needs a delegation configuration change) adds BRANCH-B-E006, gated by E002. No main-plan epic depends on the branch epic; work depending on the routing *outcome* depends on the Investigation (E002) itself. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E004 |\n| HLR-2: Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E004 |\n| HLR-3: Fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E004 |\n| HLR-4: Single-tenant / no-workspace deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E004 |\n| HLR-5: Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E005 |\n| HLR-6: Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E005, plus BRANCH-B-E006 conditionally (if the routing determination selects server-side delegation) |\n| HLR-7: Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking stores | P1 | E003 |\n| HLR-8 (P2): Audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs | P2 | E004 (bundled) — audit logging rides the same request-denial code path as HLR-2's HTTP 403 handling; a separate epic would instrument the same call sites twice, and the telemetry-channel choice (open PM/Security question) would have to be answered twice. Not separately schedulable. |\n\nAll 8 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Priority Inheritance\n\n- E003 maps to HLR-7 (P1) but enables the P0 server work (HLR-1..4) → **P0 by inheritance**.\n- E001 gates E003/E004 (P0) → **P0**.\n- E002 gates only P1 work (E005, branch epic) → **P1**.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and already exist (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy); none has uncertain timing, so there is no Tier 1/Tier 2 split on external-dependency grounds. Natural sequencing still applies: the P0 server-side capability (E001→E003→E004) delivers independent value (workspace-validated artifact serving on a manually deployed artifacts-only instance) ahead of the P1 operator topology (E002→E005), which automates its deployment.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and was parsed first. All three directives are already incorporated into the strategy's Technical Approach as written; the decomposition follows them and logs them for traceability:\n\n1. **No new flag — combine existing flags**: no `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag or `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field appears anywhere in this decomposition. E004's scope is flag *composition* of `--artifacts-only` with `--enable-workspaces`; E005 configures the existing flag set.\n2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle**: E005 is scoped as topology work (dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute, artifact traffic routing), not a CR-field/Helm-values propagation change.\n3. **SSAR detail is over-specified**: no epic dictates SelfSubjectAccessReview or other Kubernetes API mechanics. E003/E004 state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure; the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are unchanged.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\nThe canonical component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) contains `MLflow` but has no entries for `MLflow Operator` or `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`. All three strategy components are owned by the MLflow Team and map to the closest canonical name:\n\n- **MLflow** → `MLflow` (exact match) — E001, E004, BRANCH-B-E006.\n- **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins** → `MLflow` — E003 (the plugin is the Kubernetes auth/workspace extension of the MLflow server; `mlflow.md` documents it as the `kubernetes-auth` plugin, v1.5.0, loaded by the server).\n- **MLflow Operator** → `MLflow` — E002, E005 (the operator manages MLflow instances; no `MLflow Operator` canonical component exists).\n\nEpic bodies state which strategy component's surface they touch, so the span is recoverable without inventing non-canonical component names.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Strategy-level process gates pending human review** (Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD, Performance Team): all listed as \"Pending human review\" in the strategy. The decomposition assumes sign-off; a late rejection (especially Architecture Review) could restructure the epic set. Not modeled as epics — these are governance gates on the strategy, not units of engineering work.\n2. **Open question — acceptable latency overhead for workspace validation** (owner: PM/Engineering): determines whether short-TTL caching is required at launch or deferrable. The strategy's risk mitigation already prescribes implementing short-TTL caching consistent with existing auth-path caching patterns, so E004 includes it as an AC; if the PM decision defers caching, E004's scope shrinks (no structural change).\n3. **Open question — audit telemetry channel** (owner: PM/Security): HLR-8 specifies server logs, which E004 implements. If PM/Security selects a different channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log), E004's bundled audit-logging scope grows.\n4. **Scope trap — artifact path resolution**: initial implementation is scoped to the S3 artifact store's `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` path structure (standard RHOAI configuration). Non-standard artifact roots are a documented limitation, not an AC. If path resolution proves insufficient even for S3 (strategy assumption \"needs validation\", folded into E001), E004's approach is rewritten.\n5. **Epic size — E005 above the ~2-week guideline**: estimated 2-3 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic (operator split-deployment topology). Kept whole because the dedicated Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templates, routing configuration, and misconfiguration validation are one reconciler/Helm-chart change over the same rendering surface — splitting would fragment the chart and duplicate reconciliation tests. **Re-split trigger**: the routing determination expands scope beyond new chart templates plus main-Deployment routing config (e.g. requires CRD spec changes or chart-architecture rework — the strategy's \"structural changes to the chart architecture\" assumption fails), or template work exceeds the strategy's 3-sprint upper estimate; at that point split CR-spec/controller work from chart-rendering work.\n6. **Priority inheritance note**: E003's HLR-7 is P1 in the strategy but the epic is P0 by inheritance because the P0 server mode builds against it. Not an inversion — a consequence of the library→consumer edge.\n7. **Integration testing** (1-2 sprints in the strategy's estimate) is distributed as acceptance criteria across E004/E005 (multi-tenant scenarios, backwards compatibility, single-tenant regression, fail-closed edge cases, split-server routing) rather than a standalone epic — it verifies other epics' deliverables and produces no independent artifact.\n\n## Branch Documentation\n\nThe routing-approach investigation (E002) has two bounded outcomes:\n\n- **Outcome A — HTTPRoute-level routing suffices**: no additional epic; main-plan E005 configures routing at the Gateway API layer. BRANCH-B-E006 is removed (`gate_failure_impact.action=remove`).\n- **Outcome B — tracking server needs a delegation configuration change**: `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006` (Implementation, P1, gated by E002) implements the tracking-server side of routing. It is a plan document attached to the Investigation, not a standalone Jira issue; no main-plan epic depends on it.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":70901,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b018caeaf481d689","agent_id":"aace295bef333efa4","session_id":"3b7a323d-7ff9-406d-be9e-97ebfbbc4b7a","uuid":"392e4e89-fa5b-4f93-8ea5-c8cee82ccd8c","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:50:35.558Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
