{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-36ce47a8-fbb0-4476-ac61-9ac21b1bc8ac","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-89c5dceaf276f6cb","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-6bd4guuy/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E002.md","content":"# Determine artifact traffic routing approach for the split-server topology\n\n## Description\n\nInvestigation resolving how the main MLflow tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to a dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology: purely at the Gateway API HTTPRoute layer, or through a tracking-server configuration change that delegates artifact operations to the dedicated instance. The strategy flags this as an open question owned by the MLflow Team; the answer determines the HTTPRoute design for the artifacts-only instance and whether additional tracking-server work exists beyond the workspace-aware artifacts-only serving mode.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Enumerate the artifact-serving API surfaces (artifact endpoints and any artifact-related paths used by clients and the UI) that must be routed to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n- Evaluate routing options against the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern (MLflow CR spec converted to Helm values, rendered through embedded chart templates, applied via Server-Side Apply) and its conditional Gateway API CRD discovery.\n- Determine authentication and header passthrough implications: kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, Kubernetes service account bearer tokens, and the `X-MLFLOW-WORKSPACE` header must behave identically for routed artifact traffic.\n- Decide between the two bounded outcomes: (A) HTTPRoute-level routing suffices with no tracking-server change, or (B) the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations.\n- Produce an HTTPRoute design sketch for the artifacts-only instance consistent with the operator's conditional CRD discovery pattern for optional dependencies.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A decision document enumerates the artifact-serving endpoints to be routed and selects one routing approach, with evidence drawn from the operator codebase (routing and Helm chart structure) and the tracking server's endpoint surface.\n- The decision explicitly states whether the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations and, if so, what configuration surface that change needs.\n- The HTTPRoute design sketch is consistent with the operator's conditional CRD discovery pattern — no hard dependency on Gateway API resources.\n- The selected approach demonstrably preserves the unchanged authentication setup (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, service account bearer tokens, workspace header passthrough).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-6 (P1)** — determines the mechanism by which the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n- Shapes the HTTPRoute design portion of **HLR-5 (P1)**.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":74586,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b018caeaf481d689","agent_id":"aace295bef333efa4","session_id":"3b7a323d-7ff9-406d-be9e-97ebfbbc4b7a","uuid":"83ff3380-3970-468c-8691-606cfec407a5","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:50:52.476Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
