{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-eb6f8457-42b6-4a7d-9439-8d86c709053d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b49d8c313426047c","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-6bd4guuy/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E004.md","content":"# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\n\nMake `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and denies artifact requests that fail workspace validation. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags (Staff Engineer input). The design is fail-closed: if workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` together with `--enable-workspaces`; require `--workspace-store-uri` when `--enable-workspaces` is set (fail to start with a clear error otherwise). With both flags set, tracking and model registry stores stay uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (initial scope: the S3 artifact store path structure `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests failing workspace validation are denied with HTTP 403.\n- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active and the workspace store is unreachable, deny requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than serving without validation.\n- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current behavior; no existing CLI arguments, API contracts, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning; single-tenant deployments are unaffected.\n- Audit logging of workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (HLR-8) — bundled here because it instruments the same request-denial code path as the HTTP 403 handling.\n- Short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions, consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path (strategy risk mitigation; the PM/Engineering latency-target decision may re-scope this to a follow-up).\n- Document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` starts successfully; started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` and no `--workspace-store-uri`, it fails to start with a clear error.\n- With both flags set, tracking and model registry stores are not initialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- With the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n- With `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user receives HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved), verified by regression tests.\n- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in the server logs.\n- Workspace membership decisions are cached with a short TTL so that per-request validation does not perform an uncached workspace store lookup on every artifact request, verified by tests.\n- Artifact path resolution to the parent experiment works for the S3 artifact store path structure, and limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)** — `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri`.\n- **HLR-2 (P0)** — artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403.\n- **HLR-3 (P0)** — fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable.\n- **HLR-4 (P0)** — single-tenant and non-workspace deployments retain current behavior.\n- **HLR-8 (P2, bundled)** — audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs rides the same denial code path as HLR-2; a separate epic would instrument the same call sites twice and is not separately schedulable.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":75940,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b018caeaf481d689","agent_id":"aace295bef333efa4","session_id":"3b7a323d-7ff9-406d-be9e-97ebfbbc4b7a","uuid":"d1cfa3c8-b990-4271-85a6-8d2b672ce414","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:51:12.451Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
