{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-892bc353d209a0f4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n3\ttitle: Determine artifact traffic routing approach for the split-server topology\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 1\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n15\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Medium\n19\tai_implementability_score: 1\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Determine artifact traffic routing approach for the split-server topology\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tInvestigation resolving how the main MLflow tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to a dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology: purely at the Gateway API HTTPRoute layer, or through a tracking-server configuration change that delegates artifact operations to the dedicated instance. The strategy flags this as an open question owned by the MLflow Team; the answer determines the HTTPRoute design for the artifacts-only instance and whether additional tracking-server work exists beyond the workspace-aware artifacts-only serving mode.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Enumerate the artifact-serving API surfaces (artifact endpoints and any artifact-related paths used by clients and the UI) that must be routed to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n34\t- Evaluate routing options against the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern (MLflow CR spec converted to Helm values, rendered through embedded chart templates, applied via Server-Side Apply) and its conditional Gateway API CRD discovery.\n35\t- Determine authentication and header passthrough implications: kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, Kubernetes service account bearer tokens, and the `X-MLFLOW-WORKSPACE` header must behave identically for routed artifact traffic.\n36\t- Decide between the two bounded outcomes: (A) HTTPRoute-level routing suffices with no tracking-server change, or (B) the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations.\n37\t- Produce an HTTPRoute design sketch for the artifacts-only instance consistent with the operator's conditional CRD discovery pattern for optional dependencies.\n38\t\n39\t## Acceptance Criteria\n40\t\n41\t- A decision document enumerates the artifact-serving endpoints to be routed and selects one routing approach, with evidence drawn from the operator codebase (routing and Helm chart structure) and the tracking server's endpoint surface.\n42\t- The decision explicitly states whether the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations and, if so, what configuration surface that change needs.\n43\t- The HTTPRoute design sketch is consistent with the operator's conditional CRD discovery pattern — no hard dependency on Gateway API resources.\n44\t- The selected approach demonstrably preserves the unchanged authentication setup (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, service account bearer tokens, workspace header passthrough).\n45\t\n46\t## HLR Traceability\n47\t\n48\t- **HLR-6 (P1)** — determines the mechanism by which the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n49\t- Shapes the HTTPRoute design portion of **HLR-5 (P1)**.\n50\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab83309557091ef2","agent_id":"ad761b13213c7f400","session_id":"3b7a323d-7ff9-406d-be9e-97ebfbbc4b7a","uuid":"82b9aa47-be30-40ad-8f34-c0e92c842c50","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:53:58.811Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-892bc353d209a0f4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
