{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa3335e7e3675801","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 1\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: -1\n17\t  external_dependency: 0\n18\t  human_process_gates: 0\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 5\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tMake `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and denies artifact requests that fail workspace validation. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags (Staff Engineer input). The design is fail-closed: if workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` together with `--enable-workspaces`; require `--workspace-store-uri` when `--enable-workspaces` is set (fail to start with a clear error otherwise). With both flags set, tracking and model registry stores stay uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n39\t- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (initial scope: the S3 artifact store path structure `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests failing workspace validation are denied with HTTP 403.\n40\t- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active and the workspace store is unreachable, deny requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than serving without validation.\n41\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current behavior; no existing CLI arguments, API contracts, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning; single-tenant deployments are unaffected.\n42\t- Audit logging of workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (HLR-8) — bundled here because it instruments the same request-denial code path as the HTTP 403 handling.\n43\t- Short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions, consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path (strategy risk mitigation; the PM/Engineering latency-target decision may re-scope this to a follow-up).\n44\t- Document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n45\t\n46\t## Acceptance Criteria\n47\t\n48\t- The server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` starts successfully; started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` and no `--workspace-store-uri`, it fails to start with a clear error.\n49\t- With both flags set, tracking and model registry stores are not initialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n50\t- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n51\t- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n52\t- With the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n53\t- With `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user receives HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved), verified by regression tests.\n54\t- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in the server logs.\n55\t- Workspace membership decisions are cached with a short TTL so that per-request validation does not perform an uncached workspace store lookup on every artifact request, verified by tests.\n56\t- Artifact path resolution to the parent experiment works for the S3 artifact store path structure, and limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n57\t\n58\t## HLR Traceability\n59\t\n60\t- **HLR-1 (P0)** — `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri`.\n61\t- **HLR-2 (P0)** — artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403.\n62\t- **HLR-3 (P0)** — fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable.\n63\t- **HLR-4 (P0)** — single-tenant and non-workspace deployments retain current behavior.\n64\t- **HLR-8 (P2, bundled)** — audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs rides the same denial code path as HLR-2; a separate epic would instrument the same call sites twice and is not separately schedulable.\n65\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab83309557091ef2","agent_id":"ad761b13213c7f400","session_id":"3b7a323d-7ff9-406d-be9e-97ebfbbc4b7a","uuid":"f1ef054f-ffb7-4ab8-a2c5-36a584df2b15","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:54:01.561Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa3335e7e3675801","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
