{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8d0a134db2549a2e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t## Review Summary\n10\t\n11\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n12\tRecommendation: accept\n13\t\n14\tContract validator: OK (all mechanically decidable rules pass: epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, Rules 7/8/10 implementation-type parallelism).\n15\t\n16\tThe decomposition cleanly separates the two strategy work streams (server-side flag composition vs operator split-server topology) into parallel chains, uses two genuine Investigations for the strategy's two material unknowns, and correctly models the routing unknown's second outcome as a conditional branch rather than a main-plan epic. HLR-8 (P2) is bundled into E004 with an inline rationale in the traceability matrix row, sharing the HTTP-403 denial code path — compliant bundling, not priority collapse. E005's size overage is recorded in Health Warnings with estimate, why-not-split rationale, and a concrete re-split trigger (watch item, not a defect). The E005 ∥ E004 relationship is correctly expressed as an end-to-end AC gate rather than a DAG edge (configuration reference, not a build-against artifact).\n17\t\n18\t## Criterion Details\n19\t\n20\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n21\tAll 8 HLRs mapped: HLR-1..4 (P0) → E004; HLR-5/6 (P1) → E005 with BRANCH-B-E006 conditionally sharing HLR-6; HLR-7 (P1) → E003; HLR-8 (P2) → E004 with an inline `(bundled)` flag and rationale in the matrix row (shares the denial code path with HLR-2 — not deferrable separately, not priority collapse). Priority inheritance verified: E001 (P0) gates the P0 chain; E003 is correctly raised to P0 by inheritance (library → consumer edge enabling HLR-1..4); E002 (P1) gates only P1 work (E005, branch); no lower-priority epic blocks P0 work. Traceability matrix present and consistent with epic HLR Traceability sections.\n22\t\n23\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n24\tNo cycles; every edge in the summary diagram has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter and vice versa (E001→E003, E002→E005, E003→E004, E002-.->BRANCH-B-E006); arrows run dependency → dependent per convention. Edges justified: E001→E003 and E002→E005 are Rule 3 investigation gates; E003→E004 is a Rule 12/13 library→consumer edge (the server builds against the plugin's resolution capability). E005 ∥ E003/E004 correctly has no edge — the rendered flag combination is a configuration reference expressed as E005's end-to-end AC, exactly the rule-compliant shape (and the absence of an E004→E005 edge is not a missing data dependency). Branch file RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006 sets both `branch: B` and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002`, with the gate target in its direct `dependencies`; no main-plan epic lists the branch epic in `dependencies`. Main-plan critical path is 3 (E001→E003→E004), consistent with the M heuristics for a strategy of this size. No konflux-onboarding/license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 pairings are vacuously satisfied (validator confirms).\n25\t\n26\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n27\tAll epics are MLflow Team / canonical component `MLflow`. The strategy's three components (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) all belong to one team and only `MLflow` is canonical; the decomposition documents this mapping in Component Mapping Notes and each epic's scope stays on a single strategy component's surface (E003 = plugin, E004 = server, E002/E005 = operator, branch = server) — no epic spans multiple components. E005 sits above the ~2-week guideline (2-3 sprints) but Health Warnings entry 5 records the estimate, the why-not-split rationale, and a concrete re-split trigger — a defensible planning trade-off (watch item, not docked, no split demanded). No scope duplication between E005 and BRANCH-B-E006 (operator-side rendering vs server-side delegation mechanism — complementary sides of one conditional outcome, not a rebuilt surface). No code/content work-product mixing.\n28\t\n29\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n30\tBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's outcome determines whether E003 is verification-only or includes the refactor (absorbed via `gate_failure_impact.action=add_remediation` with a populated fallback); E002's outcome rewrites E005's routing design (`action=rewrite`) and selects/removes the branch epic. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (plugin capability, server mode, operator topology, delegation config). Every `gated_by` is paired with a complete `gate_failure_impact` (action + fallback_approach), every Investigation dependency carries `gated_by`, and every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` list (E003→E001, E005→E002, branch→E002).\n31\t\n32\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n33\tType-appropriate signal fields throughout: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals`, E003/E004/E005/branch carry `ai_signals`; no cross-type contamination. Signal rationales in the ai-signals files match the frontmatter values and are justified against the strategy and architecture context. Calibration spot-checks: `architecture_claims: 1` on E003/E004 cites `mlflow.md` and on E005 cites `mlflow-operator.md` — both files exist in `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/` and cover the claimed premises (kubernetes-auth plugin v1.5.0, Dockerfile.konflux CMD flags; Helm-in-operator with Server-Side Apply and conditional CRD discovery). E003's `repo_access: 0` honestly reflects that the plugin repo lacks an architecture-context file. The branch epic's 0-valued specificity/precedent/adapter signals are correct — the delegation mechanism is undefined until the gate resolves. E002's `local_runnability: 0` is defensible: the determination is answerable from source but